contentText:
With just a week to go before the Aug. 21 release of Sam Smith &#8216;s fifth studio album, Hazel Eyes , the singer told The New Yorker Radio Hour podcast this week that they&#8217;ve already begun work on LP6, and it sounds like a rager.

Unlike Hazel Eyes , which Smith said was recorded using live performers singing and playing instruments in the studio, the unnamed follow-up came about because Smith was just &#8220;inspired&#8221; to go in a different, equally alluring direction. &#8220;It&#8217;s more electro and more intense,&#8221; they promised. &#8220;I just make all different weird sounds and scream into the microphone and free myself,&#8221; said the singer known for lush, near-operatic vocals on such soaring, heartbreak ballads as &#8220;Stay With Me&#8221; and &#8220;Too Good at Goodbyes.&#8221;

But Smith also reached a chart peak with the ravenous, banging &#8220;Unholy&#8221; with Kim Petras in 2022, which hit No. 1 on the Billboard Hot 100 chart, marking a chart peak for both acts. Describing the unbound nature of the next album, Smith said sometimes those wild screams and odd sounds, &#8220;get turned into wild things you would never imagine.&#8221;

Like Hazel Eyes , Smith said the upcoming collection is also about falling in love and being in love with their fiance , fashion designer Christian Cowan, but in a &#8220;more naughty, playful way.&#8221; They described Hazel Eyes as &#8220;this deep confession, a new traditional queer love romance album, that&#8217;s how I see it. And whilst I&#8217;ve been making that, I&#8217;m also really hungry to shake my t&#8211;ties and encapsulate &#8230; the way I feel in my relationship where I feel &#8230; I&#8217;m still 34 and I love to dance and I&#8217;m free and I&#8217;m still inspired, like, intense hyper pop electro sounds. And so I&#8217;m also doing that too.&#8221;

Hazel Eyes is the follow-up to 2023&#8217;s Gloria , which ran up to No. 7 on the  Billboard 200  and featured Petras collab megahit “Unholy.&#8221;

